{"result": {"response": {"id": "chatcmpl-ETWw8UEufaef74ERyP9ncZ4umsGuA", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Controlled Rolling, Deformation, and Steel Grain Refinement\",\"summary\":\"This community comprises the thermomechanical processing concepts, equipment, temperature regions, deformation mechanisms, and steel grades associated with controlled rolling. DEFORMATION and ROLLING are central processes whose effects depend on temperature, reduction, pass sequence, and austenite recrystallization. Rougher rolling generally operates in the recrystallization region to refine austenite, while finisher rolling operates in the non-recrystallization region to accumulate strain, form deformation bands, and refine ferrite. The community also includes two-phase-region processing, strain-assisted transformation, dislocation hardening, texture development, accelerated cooling, and hot-strip-mill routes. The evidence supports substantial technical importance for controlling grain structure, strength, toughness, transition temperature, and texture, but provides no information about legal compliance, organizational ownership, or external reputation.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Deformation and controlled rolling form the community’s technical core\",\"explanation\":\"DEFORMATION is described as a central component of hot working and controlled rolling. Its outcomes depend on imposed strain, deformation temperature, rolling sequence, and the recrystallization state of austenite. Processing may occur in recrystallization, non-recrystallization, recovery, or two-phase regions, with each region affecting microstructure and mechanical properties differently [Data: Entities (116); Relationships (92, 155, 1241)].\\n\\nROLLING is likewise defined as successive deformation passes deliberately managed through temperature, strain, and pass schedules. The community therefore represents an integrated process-control framework rather than a single material or piece of equipment [Data: Entities (360); Relationships (761, 1241)].\"},{\"summary\":\"The non-recrystallization region is the principal refinement stage\",\"explanation\":\"The non-recrystallization region enables deformation while suppressing austenite recrystallization. Retained strain produces deformation bands and promotes strain-assisted transformation, while the resulting deformation structure contributes to refinement of the transformed ferritic grain structure [Data: Entities (114); Relationships (92, 679, 557)].\\n\\nFinisher rolling and final reduction are specifically associated with this region. A cited schedule used approximately 50% total reduction during finisher rolling, and the short interpass time makes the accumulated deformation approach a single-pass reduction [Data: Entities (944, 178, 984); Relationships (1282, 188, 1320, 1355)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Recrystallization-region roughing refines austenite\",\"explanation\":\"Rougher rolling applies heavy reductions in the recrystallization region. Repeated recrystallization can produce fine-grained austenite, although the refinement reaches a limiting value beyond which additional cycles provide no significant improvement [Data: Entities (943, 176, 271); Relationships (1281, 285, 453)].\\n\\nThe evidence distinguishes this stage from finishing: roughing promotes recrystallized grain renewal, while finishing retains deformation for subsequent ferrite refinement. Reduction per pass is important because successive reductions above the relevant recrystallization threshold can produce a uniform and refined structure [Data: Entities (379, 372); Relationships (481, 467)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Reduction and deformation-band density strongly influence final structure\",\"explanation\":\"Reduction affects recrystallization, grain structure, deformation-band formation, mechanical properties, and transition temperature. Deformation-band density rises only slightly below approximately 30% reduction and increases rapidly above that level, while the apparent austenite grain-boundary area becomes steady above approximately 30% reduction [Data: Entities (342, 233, 478); Relationships (614, 613, 616)].\\n\\nHeterogeneous deformation-band distribution can contribute to a mixed ferritic grain structure, whereas greater deformation in the non-recrystallization region generally supports finer ferrite grains through enhanced nucleation [Data: Entities (517, 233); Relationships (255, 679)]. The evidence therefore indicates that reduction must be coordinated with temperature and pass scheduling rather than maximized indiscriminately.\"},{\"summary\":\"Temperature scheduling controls recrystallization and transformation behavior\",\"explanation\":\"Deformation temperature determines recrystallization kinetics, critical deformation requirements, deformation-band density, transformation behavior, and final grain size. Recrystallization is described as virtually suppressed below approximately 950°C, while deformation-band density decreases rapidly above approximately 1000°C [Data: Entities (70, 359, 479); Relationships (446, 615)].\\n\\nRange 1 is associated with deformation above 1000°C and rapid recrystallization, whereas delays between regions help ensure sufficient deformation in the non-recrystallization range. The Aᵣ3 temperature also increases with greater reduction in that region, particularly for larger prior-austenite grain sizes [Data: Entities (172, 177, 616); Relationships (171, 185, 792)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Two-phase processing affects strength, toughness, and texture\",\"explanation\":\"The two-phase or austenite–ferrite region contains both γ and α phases. Deformation of C–Mn and niobium-free steels in this region is associated with improved strength and toughness, and the evidence states that heavy deformation does not impair toughness for the cited niobium-free steel [Data: Entities (555, 1038, 1039, 573); Relationships (1462, 1463, 763)].\\n\\nDeformation in this region also produces a cold-rolled texture through deformation of the α phase. However, the relationship between deformation temperature and transition temperature is explicitly described as disputed in the cited material, so the evidence does not support a single universal effect [Data: Entities (619, 555); Relationships (794, 822)].\"},{\"summary\":\"The community includes industrially capable hot-strip-mill routes\",\"explanation\":\"The hot-strip mill can apply reductions per pass exceeding the critical reduction required for completion of recrystallization. This capability is associated with a finer and more uniform austenitic grain structure than the plate mill [Data: Entities (889, 909, 906); Relationships (1225, 1226, 1245)].\\n\\nThe hot-strip-mill process route includes rougher and finisher reductions, rapid cooling on the run-out table, and slower cooling after coiling. Accelerated cooling after deformation is also identified as a controlled-rolling process stage [Data: Entities (930, 957, 1172, 966); Relationships (1252, 1291, 1701)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Strength, toughness, texture, and precipitation are linked outcomes\",\"explanation\":\"Dislocation hardening contributes to material strengthening, and its relative contribution can increase transition temperature. Deformation in the non-recovery region also contributes to increased strength, while formation of a subgrain structure just below Aᵣ3 is described as eliminating large dislocation hardening [Data: Entities (758, 1055, 845); Relationships (1102, 1492, 1106)].\\n\\nThe community further connects deformation with accelerated Nb(C,N) precipitation and connects weak textures with reduced separation or indistinct anisotropy. In the cited conditions, more than 30% total reduction below 900°C and finish rolling below 800°C were associated with improved notch toughness [Data: Entities (459, 752, 631, 77, 109); Relationships (588, 978, 842, 65, 63)].\"}],\"rating\":8.0,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community has high technical and industrial impact because it integrates hot-strip-mill processing, temperature and reduction control, microstructural refinement, and consequential strength and toughness outcomes.\"}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1790706828, "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1687, "prompt_tokens": 9869, "total_tokens": 11556, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 86, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 9866, "cached_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_tokens": 9866}}, "formatted_response": null, "obfuscation": "BTJkRdZvGmjNQH", "content": "{\"title\":\"Controlled Rolling, Deformation, and Steel Grain Refinement\",\"summary\":\"This community comprises the thermomechanical processing concepts, equipment, temperature regions, deformation mechanisms, and steel grades associated with controlled rolling. DEFORMATION and ROLLING are central processes whose effects depend on temperature, reduction, pass sequence, and austenite recrystallization. Rougher rolling generally operates in the recrystallization region to refine austenite, while finisher rolling operates in the non-recrystallization region to accumulate strain, form deformation bands, and refine ferrite. The community also includes two-phase-region processing, strain-assisted transformation, dislocation hardening, texture development, accelerated cooling, and hot-strip-mill routes. The evidence supports substantial technical importance for controlling grain structure, strength, toughness, transition temperature, and texture, but provides no information a